Huge financial imbalance: Van Duyne has $2.9M on hand vs. Burge's $37K", "Polymarket prices Democratic win chances near 9%"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point firmly to Van Duyne: an R+8 district, incumbency, a roughly 12-to-1 cash advantage, and no outside spending against her. Expert ratings lean Safe R and markets price her near 90%, so a modest shift toward the Republican from the combined baseline is warranted, though the absence of polling keeps uncertainty elevated.", "watch": "Any late surge in Democratic spending or outside opposition in this Dallas-area swing-trending district could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Beth Van Duyne (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Beth Van Duyne seeks another term in Texas's 24th District, a suburban Dallas-Fort Worth seat that leans Republican by about eight points. She enters the general election with a dominant financial position, roughly $2.9 million on hand, while Democratic nominee Kevin Burge, who emerged from a primary runoff, has raised only about $210,000 with minimal cash remaining. No outside groups are spending against the incumbent.\n\nRaters across the spectrum call the race Safe or Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Burge roughly a one-in-ten chance. With no public general-election polling available, the forecast rests on district fundamentals, money, and expert consensus, all of which favor the incumbent, though the district's suburban volatility keeps some uncertainty in play.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0657, "margin": -9.69}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0832, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0803, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.066, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0645, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0552, "margin": -10.0}]}